# -*- mode: org -*-
#+TITLE: The queue for rcc
#+STARTUP: overview
#+OPTIONS: toc:nil ^:nil
#+TODO: TODO WAITING | DONE

The queue, one chapter per part of the tree, in the order the work is
wanted; within a chapter, priority order top first. Each entry states
the fact, the fix and the check that settles it. A =WAITING= entry is
investigated and reported and waits on its owner; it leaves when the
fix it waits for lands and its check passes. Finished entries leave;
git keeps them.

#+TOC: headlines 2

* The back end

The direction is taken: c1 is rcc's only back end, it writes x86-64
and aarch64 assembly itself, and the system cc preprocesses,
assembles and links. The reasons are =docs/findings.md= and
=docs/llvm.md=, the design is =docs/backend.md= with its calls made
in =docs/design-calls.md=. The chapter holds c1's own defects and the
second target, whose design is =docs/aarch64.md=.

** TODO aarch64

The design is =docs/aarch64.md=; its chapter 15 has the nine calls
and their answers. The target takes ten register variables, =x19= to
=x28=, and the oracle's programs run on an aarch64 machine, with no
emulator. Slice 0, the ground, is in the tree: the target's
description, =-target=, the generator's files and the pins per
target. Slices 1, 2 and 3, integers, control and calls with the
frame of chapter 5, then pointers, arrays and string literals, then
register variables with the access that steps a register pointer
(=docs/autoincrement.md=), are in the tree with their pins, their
probes and their documents.

What remains: the measurement on the aarch64 machine, with the tuned
sources as first written; the loop-head alignment probe; and the
reconciliation of the two generators' register stacks (=aarch64.md=
chapter 12).

Check: =make test= on the aarch64 machine, every corpus row with
the statuses of =tests/corpus/expected= and every pin read against
=docs/aarch64.md=.

** TODO aarch64: a stack parameter at the frame's limit is refused

A parameter past the eighth is copied to its slot from above the
frame, at =[x29, #T + 8(i - 8)]= with =T= the frame's size
(=aarch64.md= 5.2). Past the load's reach the address is formed by
an =add= into =x16=, whose immediate is 12 bits shifted by 12 (5.3),
so it stops short of 16 MiB. The frame's own limit is 16 bytes under
16 MiB, and a frame there puts the eleventh parameter at 16 MiB:
~long f(long a, ..., long k) { char pad[16777096]; return k; }~, of
eleven parameters, stops the generator with "not yet: a stack
parameter 16 MiB or more above the frame record". With the array 16
bytes smaller it compiles.

Fix: for a displacement the =add= does not encode, the displacement
as a constant in =x16=, by the rule for constants, and =add x16,
x29, x16=. 5.2 loses its sentence on the limit.

Check: the program compiles and its listing assembles; run with the
stack limit raised, it returns its eleventh argument.

** TODO aarch64: a call takes 510 arguments past the eighth

The outgoing area of a call is opened by one =sub sp, sp, #A=, whose
immediate is 12 bits, and 8 bytes an argument rounded up to 16 make
the largest area 4080 bytes: 510 arguments past the eighth, 518 in
all. A call with more stops the generator with "not yet: an outgoing
area of 4096 bytes or more". x86-64 has no such limit, since it
pushes. Under 4096 bytes every store to the area is in reach at
every width, so the one limit covers the =sub= and the stores;
5.2.5.2 asks for 127 arguments in one function call.

Fix: the area opened and closed in two parts, as the frame is, and a
store past its instruction's reach (4095 bytes for =strb=, 8190 for
=strh=, 16380 for =str= of a =w= register, 32760 of an =x=) through
=x16= formed from =sp=. Chapter 8 loses its sentence on the limit.

Check: a call with 520 arguments to a function compiled by =clang=
returns the sum that tells the positions apart.

** TODO x86-64: =m[0]= of a row that is no scale adds 0

The address of a subscript by a constant 0 is the bare base, as
=*p='s is, and no instruction: =&p[0]= is =p= (=backend.md= 6.3, the
=subscript-address= row). c1 for x86-64 gets there through the
addressing form, which it uses only for a size of 1, 2, 4 or 8. A
row of another size goes through the =p + n= row, so on =int
m[2][3]= the decay of =m[0]= is =leaq -24(%rbp), %rax; addq $0,
%rax=, where on =long m[2][1]= it is the =leaq= alone. The aarch64
generator writes no instruction for either.

Fix: in =c1-x86-64.cc=, the address of a subscript by a constant 0
is the base whatever the size.

Check: a cost program with =m[0]= on =int m[2][3]=, pinned for both
targets, with no =add= after the array's address.

* Guarantees

The promises in =docs/guarantees.md=. The entry number is the
guarantee it belongs to. All are decided; G15 is withdrawn with its
number reserved. c1 writes none of the guard of G4, the round trip
of G8 and the laundering of G16, which draft 1 designed against an
optimizer, because nothing between the source and the instruction
reasons about the program.

** TODO No document gathers the implementation-defined choices

4p10 asks for "a document that defines all implementation-defined
and locale-specific characteristics and all extensions". rcc fixes
each choice per target in its code, and the documents record them
piecemeal: plain =char='s signedness on aarch64 in =docs/aarch64.md=,
the extent of =register= in =docs/cost.md= chapter 1, the narrowing
and the right shift of a negative value in G2 and G3.
=docs/guarantees.md= and =docs/rationale.md= say that no table
gathers them.

Fix: one table, per target, of the items of Annex J.3 that the
implemented subset reaches, each with its choice and the code or pin
that fixes it; the "Not in this list" of both documents points at it.

Check: every J.3 item the subset reaches has a row for each target,
and each row agrees with a pin or a probe.

* Cost model

The record in =docs/cost.md=: what each construct compiles to under
c1, its table written from the pins of =tests/cost/=. The findings of
the measurement (=docs/findings.md=) were facts about LLVM's code
generator and left with it; that page's chapter 8 says what became
of each. The entries below are rows the table lacks.

** TODO No row pins a multiply or an unsigned division by a power of two

c1 writes =x * 4= as =imull $4, %eax, %eax=, and an unsigned =x / 4=
and =x % 4= as =movl $4, %ecx; xorl %edx, %edx; divl %ecx=, the
remainder then moved from =edx= (=backend.md= 6.4). This is the
thesis's answer to finding 2 of =findings.md=, the selector's
strength reduction. But no cost program pins the three: =mul-three=
multiplies by 3 and =div-four= divides a signed value, so a change to
c1 that turned the three into a shift or a mask would show in no
row.

Fix: a cost program pinning =imull $4=, the =movl $4= into a
register and the =divl= of the unsigned division and remainder.

Check: the row and its pin.

** TODO No aarch64 pin shows a signed byte

Plain =char= is unsigned on aarch64 (=aarch64.md= chapter 11), so
every =char= of the cost corpus is loaded there with =ldrb= and
extended with =uxtb=: =cast='s =signed_()= and =narrow='s =f()= show
the unsigned forms on that target. No cost program declares a
=signed char=, so no aarch64 pin has =ldrsb= or =sxtb=, and a change
to the generator's signed byte load or extension would turn no row
red. The probes run the type; the pins do not show its listing.

Fix: a cost program with a =signed char= local, parameter and call
result, pinned for both targets.

Check: its aarch64 pin has =ldrsb= and =sxtb=, and perturbing either
in =c1-aarch64.cc= turns the row red.

** TODO Chapter 2 has no row for unary minus and complement

Chapter 2 of =cost.md= has no row for =-a= or =~a=. A negative
constant is not one of them: the parser folds =-1= to a constant, so
=long x = -1= is one store, =movq $-1, -8(%rbp)=, as =long x = 2= is
=movq $2, -8(%rbp)=, and =p[-1]= is a displacement. On a variable the
two operators are instructions, =negl %eax= and =notl %eax=, the row
of =backend.md= 6.3.

Fix: a row for =-a= and =~a=, with a cost program.

Check: the row and its pin.

** TODO The tl;dr says a register variable is never stored

=docs/cost.md='s tl;dr has a =register= variable "never stored", and
=README.md= has "a variable that is never stored". The variable has
no slot and no store at a write. But its register is stored to a
spill slot before a branch one of whose paths writes it while a read
of it is held (=backend.md= 6.7): =movq %rbx, -24(%rbp)= and =str
x19, [x29, #32]= in the two pins of =g13-read-across-branch=.

Fix: "has no slot of its own" in both places, with =backend.md= 6.7
named for the save.

Check: the sentence agrees with both pins of =g13-read-across-branch=.

* Tests

** TODO Nothing pins a diagnostic

The two corpora hold programs that build: the oracle runs them and
both scripts diff their listings. No test compiles a program that
rcc must refuse and reads the message, the position and the exit
status. The parser has 58 calls of =fail()= and 33 of =not_yet()=,
and c1 five of its own =not_yet()=; a message reworded or lost, or
a refusal that became an acceptance, changes no row. The refusal of
a local of 2 GiB or more was checked by hand when it was written.

Fix: a third corpus, =tests/errors/<name>.c=, each program with the
first line rcc must print and its exit status beside it, and a
script as the other two are, run by =make test=. The first two
programs: a local of 4 GiB, which the parser refuses at the
declaration with status 1, and two locals of 1.5 GiB, which c1
refuses with status 3.

Check: the script fails when one message of the parser is reworded
by hand, and passes when it is restored.

** TODO =tests/oracle --pin= says nothing of a pin that is the same

=tests/costs --pin= prints a line for every pin, new, changed or the
same. =tests/oracle --pin= prints one only for a new or a changed
pin, so a clean run adopts in silence and the two scripts read
differently.

Fix: the oracle prints "pinned <file> (same)" too.

Check: =tests/oracle --pin= on a clean tree prints one line for each
program.

** TODO The oracle's time limit cannot tell 124 from a timeout

GNU =timeout= exits 124 for a run it stopped and passes the program's
own status otherwise, so a program that exits 124 by itself is
recorded as =timeout=. And =timeout= sends SIGTERM, which a program
may ignore; =timeout -k= would follow with SIGKILL.

Fix: =timeout -k 1 10=, and a corpus rule that no program exits 124.

Check: a program that ignores SIGTERM ends its row.

** TODO A signal death records as 128 + n, like an exit with 128 + n

A program killed by signal n is recorded as 128 + n: =timeout= dies
of the child's signal, and the shell reports 128 + n. So the G9
program's SIGSEGV is the row's 139, and a program that exits 139 by
itself records the same.

Fix: show a status above 128 by its signal name, =SEGV= from =kill
-l=, in the table and in =expected=, and a corpus rule that no
program exits above 128.

Check: the =g9-null-load= row reads =SEGV= and passes.

** TODO A signal death leaves a core file in /tmp

The oracle runs each program with the caller's core limit. Where it is
unlimited and =core_pattern= is =/tmp/core.%e.%p.%s=, as on this
machine, every run of =g9-null-load= under rcc leaves a core of about
290 KB in =/tmp=, and =timeout= says "the monitored command dumped
core" in the log.

Fix: =ulimit -c 0= in the script before the runs.

Check: =make test= leaves no new =/tmp/core.*=.

** TODO A G14 corpus program needs a non-constant condition

=for (;;);= hangs under =clang -O2= as well: C11 6.8.5p6 exempts a
loop whose controlling expression is constant from the
forward-progress assumption. The divergence G14 promises shows only
on a loop like =while (x);= with =x= a non-volatile object the loop
never writes.

Fix: write that program as the G14 entry, expected =timeout= under
rcc and 0 under clang.

Check: the oracle row.

** TODO The register rows pin only part of c1's register rules

The five programs that declare =register= pin the registers in
declaration order, the pushes with their unwind offsets, a register
parameter's move from its argument register, a read as an operand, a
copy before an instruction that writes, an assignment, a constant's
immediate, and G5's unwritten register. No program of either corpus
shows the rest of =backend.md= chapter 4, so a change to any of these
shows in no pin:

- a compound assignment or a =++= on the register in place, =addl $1,
  %r13d=;
- a narrow register variable: the extending store =movsbl %al,
  %ebx=, the re-extension after a step, and an unwritten one's
  extension at its declaration;
- the copy of a held read before a write, =i + i++=, and before the
  paths of =&&=, =||= and =?:= (G13);
- a register parameter past the sixth, loaded from its stack slot,
  and the order of the moves and the stores among mixed parameters;
- the epilogue's =leaq -8k(%rbp), %rsp= before the pops, which only a
  function with pushes and a =subq= writes;
- the sixth live variable in a slot, and a later block taking =rbx=
  again.

Fix: a cost program for each, with its pin.

Check: perturbing each rule in =c1-x86-64.cc= turns a row red.

** TODO =-o -= names a file, not standard output

=rcc -S -o -= writes a file called =-= in the working directory. Every
cc reads =-o -= as standard output for =-S=, and a reader of the
assembly expects it.

Fix: with =-S=, an output of =-= writes the text to standard output;
with =-c= or a link it stays a usage error.

Check: =rcc -S -o - x.c | head -1= prints the first line of the
assembly and leaves no file.

** TODO Two corpus programs describe the x86-64 listing only

The comments of =g7-ptr-equal= and =g11-misaligned= in =tests/corpus=
say what c1 writes by x86-64's names: "adjacent slots off rbp", and
"by one movl: x86 has one mov for every alignment". Each has an
aarch64 pin beside its x86-64 one, with slots off =x29= and an =ldr=,
and the comment is true of one pin of the two. =g5-register-undef=
and the three =g13-= programs name both targets' instructions.

Fix: each comment names both targets' instructions, as those four
do.

Check: no comment of =tests/corpus= names a register or an
instruction of one target alone.

* Parser

** TODO A warning recorded before an error is never printed

The parser records a warning in the unit, and the driver prints the
unit's warnings only after a successful parse. =error_t= ends the
parse at the first error, so the unit and its warnings are lost with
the exception. Thus =&k= on a sixth register variable reports the
error alone: the warning that put =k= in a slot was recorded at its
declaration, before the error, and is never printed.

Fix: the driver prints the warnings the parser recorded before the
error too. Since the unit is lost with the exception, the warnings
need a home the exception does not destroy, such as a list the
driver passes to the parser.

Check: =int f(void) { register int a, b, c, d, e, k; return *&k; }=
prints the warning at =k= and then the error at =&=, exit status 1.

** TODO Assignment to a const object is not diagnosed

The parser accepts =const= and drops it: the type is the machine's
view and carries no qualifier. So =const int c = 1; c = 2;= compiles,
though 6.5.17.1p2 requires a modifiable lvalue and 6.3.2.1p1 excludes
a const-qualified one; a conforming compiler must diagnose it. The
same silence covers a =const= pointee assigned through, and a
=T *= initialized from a =const T *=.

Fix: qualifiers on the symbol and on the pointee, checked by
=require_modifiable_lvalue= and by the assignment conversion; the
type itself stays unqualified.

Check: the three programs above fail with a diagnostic; =const= in
every other position still parses.

** TODO GNU extensions in system headers reach the parser as identifiers

`clang -E` of a program that includes `stdio.h` or most other glibc
headers leaves `__attribute__`, `__extension__`, `__restrict`,
`__inline`, `__asm__` and their arguments in the text; the lexer
hands them over as identifiers and the parser refuses the first.
`limits.h` has none, so the first slice does not meet them. Any
corpus program that calls into libc will. The same family: `_Bool`,
`bool`, `inline` and every keyword outside =tok_t= arrive as
identifiers and fail as undeclared names rather than as `not yet`.

Fix: decide between skipping the extensions in the parser (an
attribute and its parenthesized argument list dropped, the others
dropped as words) and preprocessing with a flag set that hides them.
A probe says which headers need what.

Check: `#include <stdio.h>` followed by an empty `main` parses.

** TODO A parameter's array size cannot name an earlier parameter

=int f(int n, int a[n])= fails with "'n' undeclared". Before the
evaluator, any size but one integer token was a not-yet; the size is
now parsed as an expression, and the name in it is looked up. The
parser declares a definition's parameters only after the whole
declarator, in =function_definition=, and a prototype's never, so no
parameter is in scope inside the list. The program is valid C: =n='s
scope begins just after its declarator (6.2.1p7), and a definition's
parameter list has block scope (6.2.1p4). The size changes nothing:
6.7.7.4p6 adjusts the parameter to a pointer, and at prototype scope
6.7.7.3p5 treats a size that is not an integer constant expression as
=*=.

Fix: a scope opened at the parameter list, each parameter declared as
its declarator completes, which a definition keeps as its body's
scope; a parameter's size that is not an integer constant expression
taken as =*=. In a definition this skips the evaluation of the size
that 6.7.7.3p5 asks for, which only a size with a side effect shows.

Check: =int f(int n, int a[n]) { return a[0]; }= compiles to the
listing of =int f(int n, int *a)=; =int g(int n) { int a[n]; }= is
still the variable-length not-yet.

** TODO A =(void *) 0= arm makes =?:= a =void *=

=c ? (void *) 0 : p= with =p= an =int *= has type =void *= in the
tree, so =*(c ? (void *) 0 : p)= fails with "indirection through a
pointer to void". 6.5.16p7 gives the result the type of the other
operand when one operand is a null pointer constant, and =(void *) 0=
is one (6.3.2.3p3); the result is an =int *=. =common_pointer()=
asks whether an operand is a null pointer constant only when it is
not a pointer. Two pointers of different types thus meet at =void *=
whenever one is a =void *=, which is p7's rule for a pointer to void
that is not a null pointer constant.

Fix: in =common_pointer()=, a null pointer constant takes the other
operand's type before the rule for =void *= applies.

Check: =int f(int c, int *p) { return *(c ? (void *) 0 : p); }=
compiles; =c ? v : p= with =v= a =void *= variable is still =void *=.

** TODO The operand of =!= is not promoted

=!E= is defined as =(0==E)= (6.5.4.3p5), and the equality operator
promotes a =char= operand to =int=. The parser builds =!c= on the
unpromoted =char=, and c1 compares it at the =char='s width, =cmpb
$0=. The result is the same, 0 or 1, but the tree does not say what
the standard says, and the listing shows a byte compare where the
operation is on an =int=.

Fix: the parser inserts the promotion cast on the operand of =!=; c1
then writes =cmpl=.

Check: the listing of =!c= on a =char= is the load =movsbl= and
=cmpl $0=.

** TODO A conditional with two void arms is refused

~a < b ? f() : g();~ with =f= and =g= returning =void= fails with
"not yet: conditional with void operands". It is valid: 6.5.16p3
lists "both operands have void type" among the pairs the second and
third operands may be, and p6 says "If both operands have void type,
the result has void type." =conditional_expression()= converts each
arm to a value and finds their common type, and a void arm has
neither. The strict form of glibc's =assert= is this expression,
~((e) ? (void) (0) : __assert_fail(...))~.

Fix: with two void arms the parser converts neither and types the
node =void=. Such a node stands only where a value is dropped, an
expression statement, the left of a comma, a cast to =void=, an arm
of another conditional, and c1 takes a conditional's arms for their
effect there; whether either generator needs more is found with the
fix.

Check: ~void f(int *p) { *p = 1; }~, ~void g(int *p) { *p = 2; }~
and ~c ? f(&x) : g(&x);~ compile under both targets and store the
arm the condition selects.

** TODO A relational operator would take two function pointers

6.5.9p2 admits two pointers to a relational operator only as
"pointers to qualified or unqualified versions of compatible object
types". =comparison()= in =src/parse.cc= checks that the two types
are one and not what they point to, so ~p < q~ on two =int (*)(void)=
would pass. No program reaches it yet: a function pointer, a
function used as a value and =typedef= are each "not yet". Pointer
arithmetic is safe; =require_object_pointee()= refuses a function.

Fix: =comparison()= refuses a relational operator on two pointers to
a function with an error, as =require_object_pointee()= does for
arithmetic.

Check: when function pointers arrive, ~p < q~ on two function
pointers fails with the error, and ~p == q~ compiles.

* Lexer

